SwirlFS v1 — on-disk format specification
=========================================

SwirlFS is the native filesystem of SwirlOS. It was designed to be small
enough to specify on one page and simple enough to implement twice (host
mkfs tool and kernel driver) without disagreement.

Layout
------
The disk is a flat array of 4096-byte blocks:

    block 0                    superblock
    block 1 ..                 block allocation bitmap (1 bit per block)
    ...                        inode allocation bitmap
    ...                        inode table (64 bytes per inode)
    ...                        data area (file contents, directory blocks)

Superblock (block 0)
--------------------
    u32  magic            0x53575246 ("SWRF")
    u32  version          1
    u32  block_size       4096
    u32  total_blocks
    u32  total_inodes
    u32  block_bm_start   block index of the block bitmap
    u32  inode_bm_start   block index of the inode bitmap
    u32  inode_table_start
    u32  data_start       first block of the data area
    u32  root_ino         always 1
    char label[16]

Inode (64 bytes)
----------------
    u32  type             0 free, 1 file, 2 directory
    u32  size             file size in bytes (0 for directories)
    u32  links
    u32  direct[12]       direct block pointers
    u32  indirect         indirect block (1024 block pointers) or 0

Maximum file size: (12 + 1024) * 4096 = 4,218,880 bytes (~4.2 MB).

Directory block
---------------
A directory occupies exactly one block, holding up to 64 fixed-size
entries:

    u32  inode            0 marks end of valid entries
    u8   type             1 file, 2 directory
    u8   namelen
    u8   rsv[2]
    char name[56]         not NUL-terminated; length in namelen

Rules
-----
- Inode number 1 is always the root directory.
- A freed block or inode has its bitmap bit cleared; directory slots of
  removed entries are compacted by moving later entries forward.
- All integers are little-endian. No journaling in v1: unclean shutdown
  may lose recent writes; the filesystem stays structurally valid because
  metadata blocks are written after data blocks.
